			<div class="content" id="post-content-2454151">
									

<p>Maybe the divers just got <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrogen_narcosis">nitrogen narcosis</a> from diving to the floor of the Atlantic 25km offshore?</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Professional&nbsp;experience&nbsp;causes&nbsp;me&nbsp;to&nbsp;nitpick&nbsp;the&nbsp;diving&nbsp;SOPs&nbsp;a&nbsp;little..</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Hide&nbsp;nitpicking</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>It seems like the diver's only contact with the surface is by wireless voice-comms. Thats good for reporting detailed information to topside, but for the safety of the diver they should use a physical tether line as well. It's more reliable for basic communication, and aids a standby diver in finding and recovering the original diver in case of emergency.<br />
I can see maybe sending a pair down for experiments (so one can hang back while the other breaches the wreck), though even then they should probably use a buddy-line.<br />
For the purposes of your Containment Procedures dives, they could be done by a single diver on just a tether-line. Because then he only needs to do a line-search and record the location of his target.<br />
I'll stop myself here from lecturing further on commercial diving procedures, save that that this task might be done in a way that's simpler and safer.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>In general, I rather like it. There are a few places where wording seems a bit off ('permanently assess communications' rather than constantly. 'exceptionally well-conserved' rather than 'well-preserved') though I'm not sure if it's wrong or just seems odd to me. So my vote is neutral for now.</p>

							</div>

								
					
